  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_CHRONICLE', origin: :published)
    The channel cut through the tribunal floor was three flipper-spans wide and filled with salt-water pumped from the berg's mid-depth, cold enough to numb a penguin's legs in seconds. The two orcas floated in it like bodies in a grave trench, restrained by kelp-rope harnesses bolted to iron stakes driven into the ice on either side. The larger one—the one with the collapsed eye socket and the stub of a left fin—had the Twilight-bells still strapped to his ribs by their kelp-leather sling. No bailiff had dared remove them.
    
    Luminary Xuzhong Pelm'Oath stood behind the judgment stone, a block of pressure-ice polished smooth by six generations of flippers. She had been chosen by lottery during the Darkening's first week, had served for four years under Thaw-Kept's emergency clause, and had not slept in two days. Her authority to convene a cross-cultural tribunal was written in a law drafted to repel orca incursions, not to try orcas under formal procedure. She was aware of this. The forty-three penguins in the gallery were aware of this. The two orcas in the channel were aware of this and did not care.
    
    "The charges," she said.
    
    Floe-Master Laizhe chelong'Hollow stepped forward. He was small for a prosecutor, which made his voice worse—it had nowhere to hide. "Trespass upon Nightshelf sovereign territory, specifically the sealed outpost designated in current records as the Drowned Ledge. Theft of the relic known as the Thrumed~ Shadow-glass from its custodial chain. Practice of unsanctioned pressure-depth ritual within berg territorial waters. And—" He paused. The gallery shifted. "—reconnaissance consistent with the advance phase of a coordinated raid pattern, in violation of the Frozen Peace."
    
    Ruibung lay in the channel with his split fin visible above the waterline. The cut was clean, surgical—someone who knew orca anatomy had done that, and recently enough that the scar tissue was still pale. His jaw sat wrong, slightly open on the left side, as though his skull had been reassembled by someone working from memory. He did not look at Laizhe. He did not look at the gallery. He watched the water around his own body with the fixed attention of something waiting to be fed.
    
    Grandfather Blood watched the Luminary.
    
    "The accused are named," Xuzhong said. "The tribunal proceeds under Thaw-Kept authority, Section Twelve: emergency jurisdiction over external threats to colony integrity. Nightshelf territorial claims are recognized; Aurora Stack procedural law governs. The accused may speak in hunt-speech. Translation will be provided by—"
    
    "I speak your tongue." Grandfather Blood's voice came up from the water like something surfacing. It was thick, deliberate, each word costing breath he was choosing to spend. "I learned it killing your cousins at the eastern spires."
    
    The gallery went very still. Xuzhong's left flipper trembled once against the judgment stone. She pressed it flat.
    
    "The prosecution may begin."
    
    ---
    
    Laizhe was good. That was the problem—he was good, and he knew it, and he built his case the way good prosecutors do: not with passion but with architecture, each fact a stone laid against the next until the weight became unbearable.
    
    "Three generations," he said. "Three generations since the Nightshelf sealed the Drowned Ledge. Thirty-seven names on the memorial wall. Ice-locks set to hold, and hold they did—until thirteen days ago, when the patrol ship *Rime-ghost* found both locks breached from outside, the upper tunnels opened, and two orcas occupying the lower passages." He turned to the gallery. "The fire-core braziers in those passages still burn. They have burned for three generations. In an era when the Stack rations fire-cores by the quarter-cycle, when chicks die in cold-snaps because the supply lines are stretched to breaking—those braziers represent not a relic but a reserve. And the accused were found beside them."
    
    He called ☽'aeterus first. The Nightshelf penguin was old, her beak scarred by forge-work, her left eye clouded with fire-cataracts. She had been a junior miner when the Drowned Ledge was sealed. She spoke about the glow—the blue light in the flooded lower passages, visible through ice-windows thick as a body, moving wrong when you looked away. She spoke about the fire-hammer with the cracked head still wedged between two support pillars, and how the Midnight Claws had sealed the records, and how someone still left offerings at the upper locks.
    
    "The records list it as geological accident," Laizhe prompted.
    
    "The records lie." ☽'aeterus's voice was flat. "The records were kept by ☽'oon' Communion sympathizers who'd been Touched so long they couldn't tell their own memories from the ice's. We all knew. The Nightshelf sealed it because something was breached that shouldn't have been, and the Claws couldn't close it, and every cycle since, the corruption gets a little worse—"
    
    "And into that corruption," Laizhe said, turning to the channel, "came Grandfather Blood. Pressure-depth ritualist. Wearer of the Twilight-bells—stolen from the Nightshelf's own deep-shrines. Practitioner of communion with what he calls the dead god. Not a scavenger; a pilgrim. Not an opportunist; an operative."
    
    He held up the Thrumed~ Shadow-glass.
    
    In the tribunal's fire-light, it was obsidian-black, nothing more. But Laizhe cupped his flippers around it, blocking the flames, and the gallery saw the veins of captured fire-light pulse through the dark—slow, arhythmic, like a dying heart. Several penguins flinched.
    
    "Stolen from ☽'slew Nisaemesius~ by Rend Kzulrora Stolen again during the Mbra∴silentiu by Ruibung. Found in Ruibung's harness at the Drowned Ledge alongside tools consistent with fire-core extraction." He set the artifact on the evidence table. His flippers were shaking. He had held it for four seconds. "Let me remind this tribunal of Breach. Of Terrace Momiou. Of how a theft became a conflagration that burned for three cycles and left spires that still weep in spring-thaw. Of what happens when orca involvement in berg affairs escalates unchecked, while penguins debate jurisdiction."
    
    He sat down. The silence held the shape of his argument like ice holds a fossil.
    
    ---
    
    Ruibung did not move.
    
    Grandfather Blood spoke.
    
    "I would like," he said, "to call Rend Kzulrora
    
    The Luminary hesitated. Laizhe started to object—orca witnesses were not recognized under Aurora Stack procedure—but Xuzhong raised a flipper. Thaw-Kept's emergency provisions were broad enough to permit it, or narrow enough to prohibit it, depending on which subsection you cited. She chose broad. The alternative was a verdict built on a proceeding the accused could call illegitimate forever.
    
    Rend Kzulrora entered the tribunal from the side channel, where he had been held separately. He moved with careful asymmetry—left side favoring the water, right side angled as though expecting a blow. The kelp-cord around his neck held twelve penguin beaks, and the gallery counted them. The wound on his side, where Plunge-Caller Oeichowei's strike had maimed him, wept a thin trail of salt into the tribunal water.
    
    His testimony came in hunt-speech, thick with guttural stops that the Aurora Stack translator struggled to render. But the substance was clear enough.
    
    "The Drowned Ledge was empty. Not sealed—empty. The ice-locks were rusted through when we arrived. They were falling inward. The Nightshelf abandoned that place; the records say geological accident because the Nightshelf *wanted* it forgotten." He paused. The weeping wound pulsed. "We went there because there was nowhere else to go."
    
    "Explain," said Xuzhong.
    
    "Two orcas. One with a split fin. One half-blind. We are not a raiding party, Luminary. A raiding party has shore-breakers, herders, finishers. We had nothing. We had what the frozen sea leaves behind."
    
    Laizhe stood. "The Shadow-glass—"
    
    "Passed through six hands before Ruibung's," Rend Kzulrora said. "Gork Slaughterend held it first and it scarred his flippers. The ☽'ofund' Guild held it and built a *shrine* to contain it—not from reverence; from fear. In whose custody was it stolen? Not ours. The chain was already severed."
    
    "And the Twilight-bells?" Laizhe pressed. "Stolen property."
    
    Rend Kzulrora looked at Grandfather Blood. The old orca floated in the channel with his ruined eye facing the gallery, and the bells hung from his ribs in their kelp-leather sling, silent, waiting.
    
    "He prays," Rend Kzulrora said. "That is what the bells are for. He prays to a god that sleeps beneath the berg, and the Nightshelf sealed the Drowned Ledge not because they feared geological collapse but because they feared what answered when the miners went too deep." The translator's voice faltered. Rend Kzulrora continued in hunt-speech, raw and unmediated. "Your own witness said it. The corruption did not come from outside. *The Nightshelf breached something.* And now you ask why an old orca with one eye and a set of prayer-bells was found near the wound you made?"
    
    The gallery murmured. ☽'aeterus, still at her witness position, touched the forge-scars on her beak and said nothing.
    
    Laizhe stood again. "The fire-cores."
    
    Rend Kzulrora went quiet. His wound wept.
    
    "Did Ruibung carry tools consistent with fire-core extraction?"
    
    Rend Kzulrora's mouth worked. Hunt-speech; formal cadence; the collision visible in his jaw. "He carried tools. I don't know what they were consistent with. Orcas carry tools to survive."
    
    "Tools to extract fire-cores from an outpost whose reserves would supply the Stack for a full cycle?"
    
    "I don't know your math."
    
    "You know his intent."
    
    Rend Kzulrora looked at Ruibung. Ruibung looked at the water. The split fin hung above the surface like a flag torn in half.
    
    "I know," Rend Kzulrora said slowly, "that he stares at dark water and sees something he lost there. I know Grandfather Blood taught us both, and what he taught was that the deep keeps faith when the surface forgets it. Whether that is a crime in your law—" He stopped. "I do not recognize your law."
    
    Xuzhong marked that. The bells on Grandfather Blood's ribs shifted as he breathed, and the faintest sound—ice cracking, or crystal under weight—moved through the chamber and did not stop.
    
    ---
    
    Xuzhong Pelm'Oath sat alone with the judgment stone.
    
    The gallery waited. Laizhe sat with his case arranged on the evidence table—the Shadow-glass dark and pulsing in its cloth, the recovery report from the *Rime-ghost*, ☽'aeterus's deposition. On the other side of the chamber, the two orcas floated in the channel, and Rend Kzulrora had not been taken back to his holding pen. He drifted between them, his twelve beaks clicking softly against each other, his wound weeping into water that carried the blood nowhere.
    
    The fire-cores. That was what it came to. Not the theology, not the stolen glass, not the jurisdictional nightmare of trying orcas under a law written to fight them. The fire-cores. An abandoned outpost with intact reserves, and two orcas with tools and knowledge and nothing left to lose. In an era when chicks froze in their shells for want of heat.
    
    But exile beyond the berg's shadow.
    
    She pressed both flippers to the judgment stone. Felt its cold. Thought about the Luminary who had stopped Grandfather Blood's final raid under this same authority and died in the counterattack. Thought about the terraces at Momiou, how certain sounds made survivors go still and quiet. Thought about the sentence that walks itself onto the ice.
    
    "Ruibung," she said.
    
    Nothing.
    
    "You have not spoken."
    
    The orca's jaw—the jaw that didn't close right—worked once. Twice. Then: "Nothing I say means anything here."
    
    It was hunt-speech, unadorned. The translator rendered it without inflection. Xuzhong heard it anyway—the blunt exhaustion of something that had spent its entire life as evidence and never once as witness.
    
    "Guilty," she said, "on the charge of trespass. Guilty on possession of stolen property. Not guilty on the charge of coordinated raid activity. The evidence supports scavenging; it does not support assault." She paused. "The sentence is permanent exile beyond—"
    
    Her voice caught.
    
    She looked at the channel. At Ruibung's split fin, Grandfather Blood's collapsed eye, Rend Kzulrora's weeping wound. At the frozen sea beyond the tribunal walls, infinite in all directions, its horizon a line that promised nothing.
    
    "—beyond the berg's shadow. Effective at next tide."
    
    Grandfather Blood laughed. It was the worst sound anyone in that chamber had ever heard—not bitter, not defiant, but genuinely amused, as if the Luminary had told a joke only the dying find funny. The Twilight-bells rang once against his ribs, and every penguin in the gallery flinched at the sound of ice cracking under weight that wasn't there.
    
    Ruibung said nothing. His jaw hung open on the left side. He stared at the dark water around his own body, and whatever he saw there—lost, drowned, stolen, or waiting—he kept it.
    
    Rend Kzulrora was already swimming toward the exit channel when Laizhe called after him. "You are released as a cooperating witness. The tribunal thanks—"
    
    The orca did not turn. The twelve beaks swung on their kelp-cord, counting debts no tribunal could adjudicate. His wound left a thin line of salt in the water behind him, and the current carried it toward the Drowned Ledge, where the fire-core braziers still burned through frozen windows for no one, casting shadows that didn't match anything solid.
  CANONICAL_CHRONICLE
  annotation "note_1771590125398_0", anchor: "Practice of unsanctioned pressure-depth ritual within berg territorial waters", text: "Laizhe's charge list is an inadvertent taxonomy of the berg's three magic sources. The Shadow-glass carries captured fire-light — he demonstrates this when he cups it from the flames and the veins pulse. The fire-core braziers are forge-heat solidified. The pressure-depth ritual is blood-communion with the deep. Three charges mapping three sources, with aurora-light conspicuously absent — the one power that requires a sky orcas never see. The prosecution is criminalizing the two-thirds of the berg's magic that doesn't require looking up. Whether Laizhe understood this architecture or stumbled into it, I cannot say. The effect is the same.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771590125398_1", anchor: "the sentence that walks itself onto the ice", text: "Xuzhong knows exactly what she is doing. Exile beyond the berg's shadow is the Walking dressed in legal language — the same compulsion that takes our own grief-broken during every Long Dark, the ones who simply go and do not return and are found frozen or not found at all. We do not call the Walking death because we cannot be certain. She cannot call this exile death either. The honesty in this phrase suggests she tried to find another word and failed.", type: :commentary, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771590125398_2", anchor: "Nightshelf territorial claims are recognized", text: "See my annotations on Grandfather Blood's trial and Ruibung's trespass charge. \"Recognized\" by a tribunal the Nightshelf did not convene, did not attend, and apparently did not object to. Three annotations and the absurdity has not diminished.", type: :correction, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771590125398_3", anchor: "both locks breached from outside", text: "The Rime-ghost says breached from outside. Rend Kzulrora says rusted through and falling inward. Salt-flooded ice-locks corrode directionally — the pattern would settle this in an afternoon. Nobody sent an ice-reader. The tribunal preferred competing stories to evidence it could verify.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771590125398_4", anchor: "The records lie", text: "☽'aeterus just told a tribunal under oath that the Midnight Claws' record-keepers at the Drowned Ledge were Touched — Communion sympathizers who could not distinguish their own memories from the ice's. If the official account was authored by contaminated minds, every Claws-kept record from that period is suspect. Not just the Drowned Ledge. Everything they sealed. The gallery heard this as atmosphere. It is the most consequential sentence anyone spoke in that chamber, and no one followed up.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771590125398_5", anchor: "twelve penguin beaks", text: "Twelve. Grandfather Blood's cord carried fourteen. Different orca, different count. Someone is keeping precise tallies, which means these are debts, not trophies.", type: :pedantic, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771590125398_6", anchor: "genuinely amused", text: "He walked into the thermal vents eventually. Brought the bells to ☽'moth Silvesiris~, set them down, and walked in without explanation. The exile was not punishment. It was permission. Xuzhong sentenced a pilgrim to his pilgrimage and called it justice, and he laughed because he understood before she did.", type: :commentary, display: :popout
